<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="2&apos;-deoxymugineic acid phytosiderophore biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-5912" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: The expected taxonomic range of the pathway includes Oryza sativa Japonica Group, and, enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Oryza sativa Japonica Group. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; Higher plants display different mechanisms of iron absorption. Dicots and non-grass monocots extrude both protons and reducing substances from the roots and increase ferric reductase activity in the root plasma membrane in response to Fe deficiency. Fe(III) in the environment is thus reduced to the soluble Fe(II) form, which is then transported into the plant. This response is referred to as Strategy I (see |FRAME: PWY-5934|). The graminaceous monocots respond to iron defficiency by secreting phytosiderophores, in a response known as Strategy II. Fe(III) in the rhizosphere binds to the siderophores and is absorbed as Fe(III)-siderophore complex into the root plasma membrane |CITS: [15781441]|. The uptake is said to be facilitated by a putative transporter for the ferric-phytosiderophore complex, not necessitating prior reduction. One such siderophore is |FRAME: CPD-7227|, which has been shown to promote growth by orchestrating iron uptake processes, both under low and high pH conditions |CITS: [9565671][10069850][25393516]|. This compound has been shown to play an important role in many species including |FRAME: TAX-4530|, |FRAME: TAX-4577|, |FRAME: TAX-4565|, and |FRAME: TAX-4513| (although in the latter it is converted to |FRAME: CPD-7231| and some modified forms |CITS: [11346963]|) ( (see |FRAME: PWY-5925|). The biosynthetic pathway for |FRAME: CPD-7227| has been determined. |FRAME: S-ADENOSYLMETHIONINE| (SAM) is synthesized from |FRAME: MET| by |FRAME: EC-2.5.1.6|. Subsequently, three molecules of SAM are combined to form one molecule of |FRAME: CPD-463| by |FRAME: EC-2.5.1.43| (NAS). The product is then converted to |FRAME: CPD-7228| by |FRAME: EC-2.6.1.80| (NAAT), and |FRAME: CPD-7227| is synthesized by the subsequent action of |FRAME: EC-1.1.1.285| (DMAS). A further series of hydroxylation steps produces related phytosiderophores from deoxymugineic acid |CITS: [Mori87][Shojima89][16667646][12231828]|. The pathway is thought to take place in root tissue in vesicles derived from rough endoplasmic reticulum. These vesicles remain swollen until the onset of phytosiderophore secretion, and become shrunken by the end of secretion |CITS: [Mori87]|. Secretion takes place in the morning in a distinct diurnal rhythm |CITS: [Takagi84]|. It has been suggested that the pathway acts in association with the |FRAME: PWY-7270 &quot;methionine recycling pathway&quot;| that occurs in the roots |CITS: [7622460]|. Rice is one of the most important crop species in the world, and its low tolerance to Fe deficiency in calcareous and marginal soils restricts the world food supply. As the genes and corresponding enzymes involved in |FRAME: CPD-7227| biosynthesis have been isolated and characterized |CITS: [9952442][10557244][11169192][16926158][18034312]|, metabolic engineering of these enzymes in Fe-deficient crops is a desired goal in agricultural biotechnology. It should be noted that alternative systems exist for the aquizition of Fe. In a rice mutant with a defective |FRAME: EC-2.5.1.43| (NAAT) enzyme, which was unable to form |FRAME: CPD-7227|, the expression of a number of genes involved in Fe(II) acquisition was grealy increased, resulting in actually higher Fe accumulation |CITS: [17951455]|.</Description>
