    <DataNode elementId="MONOMER-13967" textLabel="3′′-deamino-3′′-oxonicotianamine reductase" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="Q0PCF5" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="318.32574462890625" centerY="666.2685546875" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="SRI, caspi, O-27, pujar">|FRAME: EC-1.1.1.285 EC 1.1.1.285, 3&amp;prime;&amp;prime;-deamino-3&amp;prime;&amp;prime;-oxonicotianamine reductase| (DMAS) catalyzes the last reaction in the pathway that produces |FRAME: CPD-7227 2&apos;-deoxymugineate|. Analysis of a rice 22-kb custom oligonucleotide DNA microarray containing the sequence data from the rice full-length cDNA project revealed a member of the aldo-keto reductase superfamily that is induced under iron-deficient conditions. A matching cDNA was isolated from a cDNA library prepared from iron-deficient roots. The |FRAME: G-10664 DMAS1| gene was cloned with a poly-His tag and expressed in |FRAME: TAX-562 &lt;i&gt;Escherichia coli&lt;/i&gt;|, and the recombinant protein was purified to homogeneity. Since the substrate for the enzyme, |FRAME: CPD-7228 3&apos;&apos;-deamino-3&apos;&apos;-oxonicotianamine|, is not available, the purified protein was assayed using |FRAME: CPD-463 L-nicotianamine| in combination with barley |FRAME: MONOMER-13941 nicotianamine transferase 1|. HPLC confirmed the production of |FRAME: CPD-7227 2&apos;-deoxymugineate| |CITS: [16926158]|. Northern blot analysis revealed that under iron-deficient conditions the expression of the gene is up-regulated in root tissue, as well as in shoot tissue. Histochemical localization analysis using DMAS1 promoter-GUS fusion transformants showed expression within the stele in the roots of iron-sufficient plants. More specifically, the gene was expressed in the parts of pericycle cells adjacent to the protoxylem and metaxylem, indicating participation in long distance transport. In roots of iron-deficient plants, the gene was active in all tissues, including the epidermis, exodermis, cortex, and whole stele |CITS: [16926158]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="MONOMER-13967" />
